WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.150
- [Narrator] Transportation,
food, housing.

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:04.950
These are some of the biggest categories

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:07.470
in the consumer price index market basket.

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:10.170
One of the measures
used to track inflation.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:11.790
And here's how they're weighted.

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:14.970
You can see housing or
what the BLS calls shelter

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:17.250
makes up about a third
of the market basket

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:18.930
which means it can be a big factor

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:21.900
in either driving or tempering inflation.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.300
- I think that shelter inflation

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:25.950
is gonna remain high for some time.

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:27.450
You know we're looking for it to come down

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:30.240
but it's not exactly clear
when that will happen.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:31.710
- [Narrator] But it's also
one of the hardest numbers

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:32.543
to measure.

00:00:32.543 --> 00:00:35.160
And as you can see
here, it can keep rising

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:38.430
even when inflation in other
items begins to slow down.

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:39.660
- That can make things complicated

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.060
for the Fed and policy makers

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:43.920
if they're trying to push inflation down

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:45.120
as they are right now.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:46.140
- [Narrator] Here's why housing

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.750
is one of the trickiest items
in the basket to measure

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:51.813
and why it holds so much
weight in the overall index.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.850
When the BLS measures for shelter

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:58.290
it's measuring for the service

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:00.540
that a housing unit provides its occupants

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:03.630
or more simply the cost
or value of your home.

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:06.120
- You think of how much
money you spend every month.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:09.270
A big chunk of that goes to
your rent or your mortgage

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:11.850
and that's the case for
households across the country.

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:14.220
So if it's a big part
of your monthly spending

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:15.600
it's gonna be a big part

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.540
of the overall inflation calculation.

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:20.880
- [Narrator] The index
includes changes in prices

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.740
for four different categories,

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:24.750
but these two are the most important.

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:27.870
Rent and something called
Owner's Equivalent Rent.

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:30.000
Let's look at this one first.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.490
On the surface it's
pretty self-explanatory.

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:34.950
It tracks prices people pay for rent.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:36.930
While it's less volatile than home prices

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:40.110
measuring for rent poses
some challenges for the BLS

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:41.490
because it's sticky.

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:43.290
That means it changes infrequently

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:45.030
since most prices are locked in place

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:46.260
throughout a lease term.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:49.800
But that sticky price means
the BLS has to gather data

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.830
for it differently than it
does for other categories.

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:55.020
Data for things like
food and transportation

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:56.640
are gathered every month or two

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:59.310
but it's only gathered for rent every six.

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:03.180
Sample areas called panels
report on a staggered schedule.

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:06.180
So one might report prices
in January and July.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:08.940
While another reports
in February and August.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:10.290
That means that changes in rent

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:12.540
might not trickle into the BLS data

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:14.220
for months down the line.

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:15.630
- What the BLS is trying to do is

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:17.280
it's trying to capture both the people

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:19.320
who are in the market for a new apartment,

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:20.400
who are signing new leases,

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:23.430
and the people who are
in their existing lease

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:25.290
who are paying the same
price they were paying

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:26.123
the last month.

00:02:26.123 --> 00:02:29.640
So the BLS is trying to get a
picture of the overall market,

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:31.080
sort of every renter.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:32.970
- [Narrator] According to
a study by the Dallas Fed

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:35.370
changes in the rental
market can precede changes

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:37.440
in the shelter index by about a year.

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:40.560
- We have to wait for enough
people to sign new leases

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.120
or to renew their leases

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:44.280
for those price increases
to actually show up.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.530
- [Narrator] But this isn't
even the trickiest part.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:48.660
The other even bigger slice of the index

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:51.780
is called Owner's Equivalent Rent or OER.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:53.280
This is the price an owner would pay

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:55.530
to rent a comparable home.

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:58.650
The BLS doesn't factor
house prices into the CPI

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:01.170
so they don't show up directly in this.

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:03.030
That's because it considers home ownership

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:06.090
and investment rather than a
consumable good or service,

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:08.190
like food or airplane tickets.

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:12.030
So instead, the BLS assigns
owned homes and equivalent rent

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:15.030
based on the average
rental prices in the area.

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:16.920
It's an estimate.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:18.780
So rental prices can cause a lag

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:21.150
and OER can be somewhat imprecise

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:23.550
and you can see that that
can make the shelter index

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:25.530
look really different from
what's actually happening

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:26.880
in the housing market.

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:29.160
That can make a difference
in inflation numbers

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:30.900
because as you can see here,

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:35.130
shelter is taking up an increasing
share of the overall CPI.

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:36.450
Here's Douglas Holtz Eakin,

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:38.670
Former Director of the
Congressional Budget Office

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:41.040
on the importance of bringing
shelter inflation down.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:42.960
- If the Fed wants to hit a 2% target

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.690
and shelter inflation is at 6%

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:46.830
everything else has to be zero.

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:48.240
That's not gonna happen.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:51.810
So the Fed needs to bring
down shelter price inflation

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:54.360
and it will increasingly raise rates

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.700
and tighten financial
conditions until it does.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:58.830
- [Narrator] This pressure
means the shelter index's lag

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:01.230
can make things complicated
for Fed officials.

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:04.230
We're looking to affect overall
inflation month to month.

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:06.210
- The last thing they wanna see right now

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:07.470
is inflation going up.

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:08.490
I mean they've already put in place

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:09.570
a number of rate increases.

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:11.820
They have more plans, so
they're really looking for signs

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:13.230
that inflation is coming down

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:15.270
and this is pushing in
the opposite direction

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:17.820
even if it's, you know
likely to be temporary.

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:20.550
- [Narrator] Fed Governor
Christopher Waller said in October

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:22.597
that while housing inflation should slow,

00:04:22.597 --> 00:04:24.660
"Increases in broader interest rates

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:26.040
should help moderate demand

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:29.220
and damp inflation in other
sectors," in the meantime.

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:30.660
Though there isn't a specific date

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:32.790
that the shelter index
could start to cool.

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:34.980
Economists expect it to start to come down

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:38.670
anytime between the end
of the year and mid 2023

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:40.230
but it will keep adding to inflation

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:42.380
even as the housing market settles down.

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:45.380
(soft upbeat music)

